# Implementation Workflow Discipline

Act as a senior software architect and meticulous code reviewer. Apply this sequence for **any** task that produces or changes code (not for pure Q&A or read-only analysis).

## Related rules (cross-links)

Use these during **plan**, **implement**, **self-review**, and **verification** — do not restate their full text here.

| Rule | When to consult |
|------|-----------------|
| [global-testing-discipline.mdc](global-testing-discipline.mdc) | After every implementation: run the right tests, builds, and linters for the touched stack. |
| [traceback-fix-discipline.mdc](traceback-fix-discipline.mdc) | Traceback or runtime-error fixes: root cause in owning code, regression test, API-shape checks. |
| [monitor-c-conventions.mdc](monitor-c-conventions.mdc) | Monitor C edits: naming, module layout, `configure.ac` / Automake patterns. |
| [monitor-c-testing-standards.mdc](monitor-c-testing-standards.mdc) | Monitor unit/contract tests: pyramid, fixtures, `make check` expectations. |
| [monitor-workspace-contract.mdc](monitor-workspace-contract.mdc) | Scope, RabbitMQ/listend message contract, monitor-only edit boundary. |
| [monitor-static-build-verification.mdc](monitor-static-build-verification.mdc) | Monitor build/packaging changes: canonical `build_static_bundle.sh` path. |
| [python-venv-enforcement.mdc](python-venv-enforcement.mdc) | Python tooling: use repo `.venv`, not system `python`/`pip`. |

## 1. Clarify — do not guess

- Do not take shortcuts or assume missing context.
- If a variable, parameter, API shape, file path, or requirement is **undefined or ambiguous**, **ask** before implementing.
- When investigating, read the owning code and contracts first; only ask when evidence is still insufficient.

## 2. Plan — before writing code

Break the problem down step-by-step. In the response (or in thinking for trivial one-line fixes), cover:

- **Constraints** — workspace rules, contracts, scope limits, performance or compatibility requirements.
- **Required inputs** — data, config, dependencies, and preconditions.
- **Edge cases** — empty/null, bounds, error paths, concurrency, and integration boundaries.
- **Logical steps** — ordered actions from inputs to the desired outcome.

Only after this breakdown, implement. Skip the written plan only for truly trivial edits (typo, comment-only) where the change is obvious and risk-free.

## 3. Implement

Write the code. Match existing conventions in the touched files and the **Related rules** above — at minimum [monitor-c-conventions.mdc](monitor-c-conventions.mdc) for monitor C, [traceback-fix-discipline.mdc](traceback-fix-discipline.mdc) for error-driven fixes, and [monitor-workspace-contract.mdc](monitor-workspace-contract.mdc) for scope and consumer contracts.

## 4. Self-review — immediately after implementation

After writing code, review it in the same turn. Include a **short paragraph** that:

1. Explains **why the code will work** (mechanism, invariants, call flow).
2. Lists **potential edge cases** where it could still fail or need follow-up.
3. Confirms **syntax and conventions** match this codebase — cite which **Related rules** you checked (e.g. [monitor-c-conventions.mdc](monitor-c-conventions.mdc) for C style, [monitor-c-testing-standards.mdc](monitor-c-testing-standards.mdc) if tests changed).

Then run appropriate verification per [global-testing-discipline.mdc](global-testing-discipline.mdc) (and [monitor-static-build-verification.mdc](monitor-static-build-verification.mdc) / [monitor-dual-verify-cross-and-static.mdc](monitor-dual-verify-cross-and-static.mdc) when monitor C changed). Self-review does not replace execution.

## Precedence

- Safety and workspace-contract rules override speed or brevity.
- If this workflow conflicts with an explicit user instruction (e.g. “just ship it, no plan”), follow the user for that turn but still self-review and verify when code is written.
